<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · 1898 · South &amp; Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="sg-title">Lupang Hinirang (Chosen Land)</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="es">Lupang Hinirang</bdi> / <bdi lang="es-Latn">Marcha Nacional Filipina</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>The national anthem of the Philippines. Its music was first played when the country declared its independence on 12 June 1898.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>José Palma (Spanish poem &quot;Filipinas&quot;); official Filipino words of 1956 by Felipe Padilla de León</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Julián Felipe</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Filipinos; the First Philippine Republic and the Republic of the Philippines</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish, Filipino, English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Kawit, Cavite, Philippines</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>music 1898; Spanish poem 1899; Filipino words 1956</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (music by Julián Felipe (1898); Spanish words from José Palma's poem &quot;Filipinas&quot; (1899))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lupang-hinirang">Lupang Hinirang</a>&quot; (&quot;Chosen Land&quot;), first titled in Spanish &quot;Marcha Nacional Filipina&quot; (&quot;Philippine National March&quot;) and often called by its opening words, is the national anthem of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>. It began as music without words. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emilio-aguinaldo">Emilio Aguinaldo</a>, head of the Dictatorial Government of the Philippines, commissioned it on 5 June 1898 for the proclamation of independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, to replace a march he had found unsatisfactory, and gave the task to Julián Felipe. First called &quot;Marcha Filipina-Magdalo&quot;, it was renamed &quot;Marcha Nacional Filipina&quot; on its adoption as the national anthem on 11 June 1898. Felipe said he had based it on three pieces: the &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcha-real">Marcha Real</a>&quot; of Spain, the Grand March from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-verdi">Giuseppe Verdi</a>'s Aida and &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-marseillaise">La Marseillaise</a>&quot;. The Banda <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a> de Malabón, now the Banda Matanda of General Trias, played it in public for the first time at the proclamation of independence at Aguinaldo's house in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kawit">Kawit</a>, Cavite, on 12 June 1898. It was adopted again as the national march of the Philippine Republic in 1899.</p><p>The words came a year later. In August 1899 the soldier and writer José <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palma">Palma</a> wrote the Spanish poem &quot;Filipinas&quot;, which was itself drawn from a Kapampangan poem, &quot;Labuad Mapalad&quot; (&quot;Blessed Land&quot;), written in September 1898 by Mariano Proceso Pabalan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bacolor">Bacolor</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pampanga">Pampanga</a>. &quot;Filipinas&quot; was first published on 3 September 1899, for the first anniversary of the newspaper La Independencia, and was then set to Felipe's march. In the years after the revolution it became widely popular as the anthem's unofficial Spanish words.</p><p>After the defeat of the First Republic in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippine-american-war">Philippine–American War</a>, the islands came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> rule. The Flag Act of 1907 forbade the public display of the flags, banners and emblems of the Philippine Republican Army; it did not forbid the march itself, yet in 1909 an entire band was imprisoned for playing it at a fiesta. When the act was repealed in 1919 the march regained its popular place as the national anthem, and the Insular Government had the Spanish words put into English: first by the poet Paz Márquez Benítez of the University of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, then in the best-known translation, the &quot;Philippine Hymn&quot; by Senator Camilo Osías and Mary A. Lane. It was played beside &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-star-spangled-banner">The Star-Spangled Banner</a>&quot; until independence in 1946. Under the Commonwealth, Commonwealth Act No. 382 of 5 September 1938 adopted Felipe's music as the national anthem; it concerned only the music. In the 1920s the anthem's time was changed from 2/4 to 4/4 and its key from C major to G to make it easier to sing, though this was never written into law.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagalog-language">Tagalog</a> versions appeared in the 1940s. The first known, &quot;Diwa ng Bayan&quot; (&quot;Spirit of the Country&quot;), was sung during the Japanese occupation; &quot;O Sintang Lupa&quot; (&quot;O Beloved Land&quot;) by Julián Cruz Balmaceda, Ildefonso Santos and Francisco Caballo became the official version in 1948. Under President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramon-magsaysay">Ramon Magsaysay</a>, Education Secretary Gregorio Hernández formed a commission to revise the words, and the Tagalog &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lupang-hinirang">Lupang Hinirang</a>&quot; was first sung on 26 May 1956. With small revisions in the 1960s, this version by Felipe Padilla de León is the one used today. Republic Act No. 8491, the Flag and Heraldic Code, passed on 12 February 1998, made these Filipino words law and set aside the Spanish and English versions: the anthem must be sung in the national language, inside the country or abroad, &quot;with fervor&quot;, and schools must see that every student learns it by heart.</p><p>Under the Marcos <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a>, until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/people-power-revolution">EDSA Revolution</a> of 1986, opposition parties, activists and unions opened their protests with the anthem, singing it with the raised clenched fist instead of the hand on the heart. The law requires that it follow Felipe's original arrangement, but his original <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> cannot be found. The National Historical Institute holds that it should be played in 2/4 time at 100 beats a minute and last 53 seconds, and has praised or criticised singers at televised boxing matches of Manny Pacquiao for going too slow or too fast. Proposals to change its last line, by the musician Joey Ayala in 2013 and Senate President Vicente Sotto III in 2018, met with mixed or hostile reactions. Earlier, in November 1896, the composer Julio Nakpil had written &quot;Marangal na <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalit">Dalit</a> ng Katagalugan&quot; as a national anthem for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andres-bonifacio">Andrés Bonifacio</a> of the Katipunan, but Aguinaldo's governments never adopted it.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A hymn of devotion to the homeland, which calls the country a daughter of the eastern sun in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palma">Palma</a>'s Spanish and ends with its people's readiness to die for it. Its mood is solemn and fervent.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Spanish</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Spanish</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Tierra adorada,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Beloved land,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Hija del sol de Oriente,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Daughter of the sun of the East,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Su fuego ardiente</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Its burning fire</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">En ti latiendo está.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Is beating in you.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">¡Tierra de amores!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Land of loves!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Del heroísmo cuna,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Cradle of heroism,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Los invasores</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The invaders</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">No te hollarán jamás.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Will never tread you underfoot.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">En tu azul cielo, en tus auras,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>In your blue sky, in your breezes,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">En tus montes y en tu mar,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>In your mountains and in your sea,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Esplende y late el poema</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Shines and beats the poem</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">De tu amada libertad.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Of your beloved liberty.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Tu pabellón que en las lides</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Your flag, which in the battles</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">La victoria iluminó,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Victory lit up,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">No verá nunca apagados</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Will never see extinguished</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Sus estrellas y su sol.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Its stars and its sun.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Tierra de dichas, del sol y amores,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Land of joys, of sun and loves,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">En tu regazo dulce es vivir.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>In your lap it is sweet to live.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Es una gloria para tus hijos,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>It is a glory for your children,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Cuando te ofenden, por ti morir.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>When they wrong you, to die for you.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: José Palma, &quot;Filipinas&quot; (1899); the Spanish text as quoted in Wikipedia's article (Spanish Wikipedia). Text from <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lupang_Hinirang" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (4)</summary><ul><li>line 1.1: 'adorada', adored or beloved.</li><li>line 2.4: 'hollar', to trample or tread on.</li><li>line 3.1: 'auras', breezes (soft airs).</li><li>line 5.4: 'Cuando te ofenden', when they (enemies) offend or wrong you.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Filipinas (José Palma)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1899</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Spanish poem set to the march, the anthem's unofficial words</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">English translation (Paz Márquez Benítez)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">c. 1919</span><span class="sg-chain-h">translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Philippine Hymn (Camilo Osías and Mary A. Lane)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">English translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Diwa ng Bayan</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1940s</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Tagalog translation, sung during the Japanese occupation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">O Sintang Lupa (Julián Cruz Balmaceda, Ildefonso Santos, Francisco Caballo)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1948</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Tagalog translation, made official</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Lupang Hinirang (Felipe Padilla de León)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1956</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Filipino words, revised in the 1960s; official since</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><a href="/songs/marcha-real">Marcha Real</a><span class="sg-chain-h">one of three pieces Felipe said he based the music on</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Grand March from Aida (Giuseppe Verdi)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">one of three pieces Felipe said he based the music on</span></li><li><a href="/songs/la-marseillaise">La Marseillaise</a> <span class="sg-chain-y">1792</span><span class="sg-chain-h">one of three pieces Felipe said he based the music on</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Labuad Mapalad (Mariano Proceso Pabalan)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1898</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Kapampangan poem from which Palma's &quot;Filipinas&quot; was drawn</span></li></ul></section>
